// Language section: References.
page references { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "References" {}

  p "A `&T` field accepts any value that structurally satisfies interface `T`. It's how a record or function declares \"give me anything that looks like this\" without committing to a specific concrete type." {}

  h2 "Declaring a reference field" {}

  p "Use `&InterfaceName` as the field type. The accepted values are exactly those that satisfy [the interface](interfaces) — every required field, with compatible types." {}
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
interface Drawable {
  x: f64
  y: f64
}

type Scene {
  focus: &Drawable     // any value with x and y of type f64
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Why a reference?" {}

  p "A reference field lets a schema be open-ended without being untyped. A renderer that consumes `&Drawable` can accept any future shape that satisfies the contract — `Circle`, `Image`, `Container`, a user's `Custom` — without knowing what those concrete types are." {}
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
type Circle { x: f64  y: f64  radius: f64 }
type Square { x: f64  y: f64  side:   f64 }

s1 = Scene { focus: Circle { x: 0.0,  y: 0.0,  radius: 5.0 } }
s2 = Scene { focus: Square { x: 10.0, y: 10.0, side:   8.0 } }
WCL
  }

  callout "Structural, not nominal" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "A type does not need to explicitly extend or implement the interface. If it happens to have all the required fields with compatible types, it satisfies — and a &T field will accept it."
  }

  p "Next: [Unions](unions)." {}
}
